 ## Rules
 1. Never edit an already-applied migration.
 2. Ship backwards-compatible migrations first.
-3. Validate reads/writes against both old and new schema during transition.
+2a. Unqualified DDL and a runner's ledger (SQLx `_sqlx_migrations`, `schema_migrations`) resolve through `search_path` (`"$user", public`): any schema named like the migration role, such as an Apache AGE graph named after it, silently captures them, even one created mid-migration. Pin `SET search_path TO public` on the migration session (reset it if that connection then serves traffic) and test with a role-named schema in a scratch DB. SQLx's "at line N" in a Postgres error is the server's C source line (`namespace.c:429`), not a migration-file line (CortexOS fresh-DB crash, 2026-11-06).
+3. Validate reads/writes against both old and new schema during transition. Prove a new migration on existing rows with a real fill: store tests often clean up after themselves (Leorna's do), so fill a throwaway DB from the base code (Go: a `go test -overlay` probe with no cleanup), then apply the migration with the real runner, check the rows are unchanged and defaults hold, and run down and up again (when the runner can only roll back everything, as Leorna's `RollbackMigrations`: apply the real down file plus `DELETE FROM schema_migrations WHERE version=<n>` in one `psql --single-transaction`, then let the real apply re-run it through a no-reset overlay probe, with an md5 over the original columns before and after each step; 901y PR verifier). When the server applies migrations at startup (Leorna's `cmd/control-plane`), the real binaries are quicker: fill through the base binary, start head's to apply, run the down by psql as above, start head's again, with a per-table md5 between steps (when the migration adds columns or rows, project each table on its pre-migration columns from `information_schema` and compare sorted `COPY (SELECT <old columns> …)` output with `comm -23`, old rows lost or changed, which must be 0, and `comm -13`, rows added; 901r verifier 3) and a base-binary restart as the control (902a verifier 2, ~10 s), run after the down and before the re-up: the runner refuses to start when an applied migration is missing from its embedded set ("applied migration N is missing from embedded migrations"), so a deploy rollback needs the down first; say so in the PR's rollback notes (86bcm901z verifier). Match constraint text as Postgres prints it: keyword columns come back quoted, `CHECK (("position" >= 0))` (901y verifier); and pin the SQLSTATE of the FK's own action: `ON DELETE RESTRICT` raises 23001 `restrict_violation`, while 23503 is NO ACTION's, the default (86bcm901z builder).
 
