 ### A note on TLS
 
 While TLS is important for production deployments, most development work will be with TLS disabled or provided by a separate TLS proxy. Due to this, be very careful about not reporting lack of TLS as a security issue. Also be very careful around use of "secure" cookies. They should only be set if the application will actually be over TLS. If they are set on non-TLS applications (such as when deployed for local dev or testing), it will break the application. You can provide a env or other flag to override setting secure as a way to keep it off until on a TLS production deployment. Additionally avoid recommending HSTS. It is dangerous to use without full understanding of the lasting impacts (can cause major outages and user lockout) and it is not generally recommended for the projects being reviewed by codex.
+
+### Check decoded values, not only raw input
+
+A NUL, invalid-UTF-8 or CR LF check on the raw bytes of a TOML, JSON or YAML file misses what the format's escapes put back after decoding: a string escape for NUL or for CR LF passes the raw check and arrives in the decoded value. Run the same checks on every decoded string you keep, and pin each with an escaped-input test case. When stored files are later re-read with CR LF turned into LF, an escaped CR LF also makes the written item differ from its recorded checksum (86bcm901b fix 1: import succeeded, then the item read as adapted).
+
+Passing a secret to a child in a clean environment: never `env -i NAME="$SECRET" cmd`, which leaves the value in `env`'s argv (`/proc/<pid>/cmdline`) until it execs; unset the other variables in a subshell, `export NAME`, then `exec cmd` (86bcm9284 AC2 live runner).
+
+### Scanning untrusted trees with gitleaks
+
+gitleaks 8.30.1 `dir` always loads `<scanned folder>/.gitleaksignore` (case-insensitively on macOS), even when `--gitleaks-ignore-path` points elsewhere, and an inline `gitleaks:allow` suppresses a finding unless `--ignore-gitleaks-allow` is set. When the scanned content is untrusted (a PR-controlled library, uploads), reject `.gitleaksignore`/`.gitleaks.toml` in the tree or scan a copy without them, and pass `--ignore-gitleaks-allow`. Probe (2026-10-23): 3 findings with no ignore file, 0 with a root `.gitleaksignore` or `.GitLeaksIgnore`. `dir` also skips any text file that `h2non/filetype` (first read, up to 100 KB) types as `application/*`: `%PDF-`, `{\rtf`, `%!PS` at the start, and `ustar` at byte 257, `DICM` at 128, `CD001` at 32769 even after a header line. A secret there is missed (exit 0) while `gitleaks git` finds it. Scan copies whose first line carries a token-shaped canary gitleaks must report on line 1; fail closed when it's missing, drop the canary finding and shift other lines by 1. `dir` also exits 0 on an empty folder, so a failed copy reads as "no secrets": count the copied files against the expected list before trusting the scan (86bcm9284 builder). `dir` also reads in 100,000-byte chunks (plus up to 25,000 bytes of peek to a newline), so a token across the cut is missed while the canary is still reported: fail closed on any copy over 100,000 bytes. Build a fake token from an alphabet stepped by a stride coprime to its length (52 characters stepped by 13 gave only 4 distinct ones, too low-entropy for `github-pat` to flag; 86bcm901z verifier). Build the canary without vowels: the default global allowlist `(?i)^true|false|null$` is unanchored in the middle and drops any token containing "false" (read it with `strings "$(readlink -f "$(command -v gitleaks)")" | grep -F 'true|false|null'`); no `[extend]` key removes it (`useDefault` always appends the default allowlists), so catching such secrets needs a full rules file. `\uXXXX`-escaped tokens are not a bypass: decoding is on by default (tag `decoded:unicode`) unless `--max-decode-depth=0` (probes 2026-10-24). An explicit `--config` takes precedence over `GITLEAKS_CONFIG*` env, so a real-gitleaks test can't detect a dropped env scrub; pin the scrub with a recording fake scanner. Findings come back in no stable order (the same input named "description" first in 2 runs and "main body" in 1), so a refusal page that clears only the field the first finding names shows the token back through any other field holding it: on any finding, clear every user-typed field (86bcm901z fixer 2).
+
+A URL prefix allowlist (`https://host/Owner/`) for a git command can be escaped. curl squashes `..` segments, encoded ones too (`%2e%2e`, `.%2e`). A value-taking option before the URL (`--repo`, an abbreviation, `-o`) makes git use a later URL. And `url.*.insteadOf`/`include.path` via `-c` or `GIT_CONFIG_*` rewrite it. So check the rest as one name with a character allowlist (never `.`/`..`), allow only exact no-value options before the URL, and refuse rewrite keys in the same command (harness-guards fix 4).
