ace warning and connects, as Node 22 fetch does; the trim is HTTP whitespace only, so a CR or LF next to a no-break space, U+000B or U+000C still counts as inside) or a character above U+00FF, or a header name is empty or holds a NUL, line break, space or colon (tab, U+0001, U+007F and Latin-1 values pass), while Codex 0.165 warns and connects without that header (any non-token name; CR, LF, NUL or any control character but tab in a value). Both send a template `Host` in place of the url's host, Codex sends a template `Content-Length` and cuts the body to it, Claude Code joins case-variant names into one header (two Bearer tokens in one `Authorization`) and expands `${NAME:-default}` (its pattern is `/\$\{([A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*(?::-[^}]*)?)\}/g`: valid NAME and closing brace only, so `${1ABC}`, `${A.B}` and an unclosed `${A:-x` go as written; header values get a second pass, a variable's own value included, so `$${EMPTY}{SET}` with EMPTY empty reads SET, while command, args, env values and url get one; it also reads `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` and `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN` as empty toward a remote server, set or unset (a stdio server gets the real value), a guaranteed empty reference: refuse `$` or an unfinished `${` directly before a reference, and `{` directly after one, in header values) in command, args, env, url and headers while Codex sends it as written. Node 22's own fetch, unlike Claude Code, also refuses U+0001–U+001F (not tab) and U+007F in a value, so Node-based clients (Cursor) may differ again. So a policy that reports a tool as verified checks all of these, not only NUL (901j fixer 4, verifier 3, fixer 6). To see what a launched server really received, have the fake command dump its own `/proc/$$/environ`: a `/bin/sh` fake re-exports only valid names, so odd keys look dropped (901j fixer 3) (901j review 2: Claude Code 2.1.312 ran a fake `npx` from env.PATH; `env -i HOME=<scratch> claude mcp add-json -s user …` then `claude mcp list` starts every user-scope s